    "growth_rate_source": "revenue_5yr_cagr rounded up for Frontier fibre and guided service-revenue acceleration (2.5-3.0% FY2026, ~4% exit rate)",
    "growth_divergence_warning": "Equity 5yr CAGR (8.8%) diverges >30% from revenue (1.5%) and EPS (-1.1%): book equity grew from retained earnings and the 2021-22 C-band balance sheet, not from a growing business. Equity CAGR is NOT used as the growth anchor.",
    "notes": "Owner FCF = OCF - cash capex - SBC (interest income 0; net debt). FY2020 22,846 -> FY2021 18,563 -> FY2022 13,434 -> FY2023 17,984 -> FY2024 19,122 -> FY2025 19,460. The 3yr CAGR starts from the FY2022 capex peak (C-band build), so it flatters; the 5yr CAGR is negative. Owner and reported CAGRs are nearly identical because SBC is ~0.5% of revenue and falling in dollars -- SBC is not growing faster than cash flow. Reported FCF excludes spectrum purchases ($47.6B in FY2021 alone); including them the 5yr record is far weaker."
  },
  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "Verizon delivers FY2026 guidance (service revenue +2.5-3.0%, FCF >= $21.5B, capex $16.0-16.5B) and reported revenue resumes ~2.5% growth from FY2027 as Frontier fibre and FWA broadband add subscribers and postpaid phone net adds stay positive under Schulman. The $5B cost programme and ~$1B Frontier synergies lift pre-SBC EBITDA from ~36% (after recurring restructuring cash) to 38.5% by FY2029, roughly the post-cut run-rate already visible in Q2-2026 (40.1% adjusted, before special items). Cash capex glides from 11.7% to 11.0% of revenue as the Frontier build matures, plus a 2% spectrum allowance. The market does not re-rate wireless: exit at 13x unlevered FCF, about today's multiple.",
    "bull": "The customer-first pivot proves durable: Verizon takes share from T-Mobile and cable MVNOs, churn falls, and convergence (mobile + Frontier fibre/FWA bundles) lifts ARPA, so revenue grows 3-3.5% for several years. Cost transformation (AI-native care, three layoff rounds) and Frontier synergies take pre-SBC EBITDA to 40%, cash capex falls toward 10.5% of revenue once the fibre build peaks, and no large spectrum auction lands in the decade. Deleveraging to 2.0-2.25x by 2027 lets buybacks scale; exit at 14x.",
    "bear": "The early turnaround metrics reverse: T-Mobile and cable MVNOs escalate promotions, Verizon buys net adds with device subsidies, and the $5B of cost savings is reinvested in price, so EBITDA margin stays ~35.5% and slips to 35%. Ex-Frontier revenue keeps contracting, holding total growth to ~1%. Frontier integration costs run long and the upper-mid-band spectrum pipeline (FCC auction authority restored 2025) forces a heavy licence buy, pushing capex plus spectrum to 14.5% of revenue. Leverage stays near 2.5x, refinancing at higher coupons squeezes the dividend; exit at 11x."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "Unlevered owner-FCF DCF of a mature, capital-intensive, highly levered telecom. Value is driven by the EBITDA margin the cost programme can hold against competition and by the true all-in capital intensity including spectrum; leverage turns small operating changes into large per-share swings, so the scenario spread is wide relative to the price.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "Equity is ~47% of EV: every 1% of enterprise value is ~2% of equity value.",
      "Spectrum allowance (2% of revenue) is a judgment; a C-band-scale auction would overwhelm it in the year it lands.",
      "Adjusted EBITDA excludes severance that has recurred annually; if restructuring ends the base margin path is conservative, if it continues the bear path is closer.",
      "Device-receivable ABS deducted in full as debt (conservative ~$4-5/share).",
      "Bonus-depreciation cash-tax benefit assumed to fade; if capex grows it persists longer."
    ]
  },
