    "wacc_rationale": "USD flows, USD rate. Risk-free 4.25% (10y UST), ERP 5.0%, levered beta ~0.75 (raw 2y beta ~0.4 understates a 46% debt/EV balance sheet; asset beta ~0.35 relevered) -> CoE ~8.0%. Pre-tax cost of debt ~4.9% (FY2026 run-rate interest expense ~$7.9B over ~$165B debt), 3.7% after 24% tax. Market weights: equity ~$191B, net debt + after-tax pension ~$172B -> D/V ~47% -> WACC ~6.0%; set at 6.5% to reflect refinancing exposure on ~$17-22B/yr of maturities. Same WACC in all three scenarios: risk is charged in the flows (probability-weighted scenarios), not stacked in the rate.",
    "lease_treatment": "US GAAP ASC 842. Operating-lease cost (towers, retail, network sites; ~$5.3B cash in FY2025) is already inside operating expense and therefore inside EBITDA, so the flows bear it; the $23.5B operating-lease liability is EXCLUDED from net debt. Finance leases are inside total debt (LongTermDebtAndCapitalLeaseObligations) and their principal is not deducted from the flows. One treatment, applied once.",
    "nci_treatment": "Flows are consolidated (100%); NCI ($1,276M book, 2026-06-30) deducted once in the equity bridge.",
    "notes": "MODEL: owner-FCF component DCF (unlevered), per dcf-methods routing -- Verizon is an operating company (wireless + wireline/fibre), not a bank/REIT/holdco; its leverage is ordinary corporate debt, so the right treatment is an unlevered DCF at WACC with the full debt stack deducted in the bridge, not a capitalisation of a post-interest flow. US filer, but owner-fcf-adaptations applied for leases (ASC 842 operating leases inside EBITDA), NCI and pension. FY0 = FY2025 revenue $138,191M; FY2026 growth path (+0.8%) is calibrated to H1-2026 actual revenue $68,693M (+1.0% YoY) INCLUDING Frontier from 2026-01-20 -- i.e. ex-Frontier revenue is contracting on lower equipment revenue while service revenue grows 2.5-3.0% (guided). EBITDA path is PRE-SBC and after a recurring-restructuring haircut: management's adjusted EBITDA (Q2-2026 40.1% margin) excludes severance/special items that have recurred in 2023, 2025 and 2026, and FY2026 guided FCF of >= $21.5B implies ~$4-5B/yr of cash costs (severance, pension, device working capital) between adjusted EBITDA and OCF; the FY2026 36.0% pre-SBC margin reproduces guided FCF (~$21.5B after ~$6B after-tax interest, before a spectrum allowance). CAPEX path = cash capex (FY2026 guidance $16.0-16.5B = ~11.7% of revenue) PLUS a normalised spectrum allowance of 2.0% of revenue (~$2.8B/yr): Verizon's FCF definition excludes spectrum, but licences are a recurring cost of staying in the business -- FY2015-FY2025 spectrum purchases totalled ~$75.7B (~$6.9B/yr including the once-a-generation C-band buy). CASH TAX on unlevered EBIT: FY2025 cash taxes $3,581M plus ~$1.4B interest shield ~= 16-17% of EBIT, depressed by permanent 100% bonus depreciation; ramps to 23% (21% federal + state) as the capex-growth timing benefit fades. WORKING CAPITAL: -5% of the revenue change (device-payment receivables absorb cash as postpaid volumes grow). BUYBACKS: an active repurchaser ($2.5B Q1 + $1.0B Q2 2026, $4.5B guided FY2026), so the flat count is funded. Total debt includes ~$20B+ of device-receivable ABS that is backed by device-payment receivables; deducting it in full while those receivables sit inside working capital is conservative by roughly $4-5/share.",
